The Ministry of Health has launched a plan to explore more TB in children to reduce the rate of infection and death to be no longer a public health problem by 2030 (Video inside) (Phnom Penh): The Minister of Health, Professor Mam Bunheng, said that to achieve the goal Set for the coming years and towards achieving the Sustainable Development Goals by 2030, the Ministry of Health has launched a plan to detect more TB in children, previously only for the elderly.
Faster detection, faster and more effective treatment.
"Our goal is to find all TB patients across the country as soon as possible so that they can be treated as soon as possible," he said. According to the policy of the Royal Government, all TB treatment to the people is free.
Minister Mam Bunheng also called on the private sector to cooperate. If patients go for treatment and are diagnosed with TB, they should be referred to a public hospital, as the disease is a long-term treatment for 4 to 6 months. Let the patient spend a lot of money.
In addition, the Minister called on people, especially the elderly who are thin, warm at night, cough more than 20 times a day, and for children as well, especially malnourished, please come to hospitals and centers. Health near you.
This study project is an international study involving seven countries: Cambodia, Cameroon, Ivory Coast, Mozambique, Sierra Leone, Uganda and Zambia, which are the most important for the research and diagnosis of tuberculosis in children that the international community has never done before. Many are waiting for the results to be implemented.
He said there were more than 1 million new cases of new TB in children worldwide by 2020, but only 400,000 new cases of new TB were reported. More than half of children and adolescents with TB Facing access to guidance due to difficulties in diagnosis and treatment.
