(Abu Dhabi): Indonesia and the United Arab Emirates last weekend signed a free trade agreement to strengthen economic ties between Southeast Asia's largest economy and the Persian Gulf oil-producing country. According to a report by Reuters.
The agreement reached during Indonesian President Joko Widodo's visit to Abu Dhabi will eliminate or reduce tariffs on most goods traded between the two Muslim countries, Reuters reported.
The UAE's Ministry of Economy says palm oil, food products, Indonesian fashion and petrochemicals, rubber, plastics and steel products will benefit from tariff reductions or abolitions.
Indonesia's Ministry of Commerce says the agreement includes a chapter on services, investment, intellectual property and mutual recognition of halal food certification. The text of this agreement has not been published and has been ratified by both countries.
Indonesia's main exports to the UAE include palm oil, jewelry and precious metals, while the UAE's exports to Indonesia are mostly gas and oil, iron ore and iron ore, according to Reuters. .
The oil-rich United Arab Emirates has forged closer trade ties with Indonesia as part of its ambition to double its economy to $ 816 billion by the end of the decade, in part through the signing of a free trade agreement. .
Indonesian Trade Minister Zulkifli Hasan said Jakarta expects more investment from the United Arab Emirates in Indonesia after the deal, which he said would boost exports to the Middle East and other countries. According to Reuters.
Crisis Sri Lanka has less than a day to run out of fuel
COLOMBO (Reuters) - Sri Lanka's energy minister said on Sunday that the country was facing "less than a day" of fuel shortages, which had disrupted public transport as the country's economic crisis worsened. Seriously. According to the AFP news agency.
Sri Lankans waiting in line to refuel in Colombo for miles, even though most gas stations have run out of gas for days.
Last week, the cash-strapped Sri Lanka announced a two-week moratorium on all fuel sales except for essential services to save gasoline and diesel for emergencies.
Most shops were closed on Sunday, with the situation expected to worsen when banks and offices reopened on Monday, the AFP news agency reported.
International Analysis: Who is winning the civil war in Myanmar?
BERLIN: Since the outbreak of the military coup in February 2021, Myanmar has plunged into a civil war of fighting between the military and armed resistance groups across the country. At the same time, there are many assessments of the situation in Myanmar, and also an important question is who is winning the civil war in Myanmar? That is, Min Aung Lan's military or the armed resistance movement?
This question is not only being asked by the people of Myanmar, but also by leaders in neighboring Myanmar and experts around the world. Over the last six months, reports, commentaries and interviews have been published in the international press, and many academics are trying to answer this question.
So the main problem is the lack of reliable information on the general situation of the conflict in Myanmar, while some information is reported by ordinary people who are difficult to verify independently. However, what is clear is that violence is spreading across Myanmar. Therefore, the question of who will win the civil war in Myanmar in the future may not have a definite answer.
