(Phnom Penh): The Documentation Center of Cambodia is committed to empowering survivors of the Khmer Rouge regime, their families, communities and the public with greater access to information about Khmer Rouge history. At the same time, DC-Cam continues to make its archives more widely available online.
DC-Cam is proud to officially announce the launch of its five regional centers representing the DC-Cam network throughout the country.
The establishment of regional centers across the country in line with the wishes of victims of the Khmer Rouge who wanted to see resources on their experiences and stories of historical periods in all parts of Cambodia.
Community Centers not only provide a rich array of multimedia resources for education and research, the Community Centers also serve as an important meeting venue for community forums, speakers, conferences, book reviews. Recall art and music exhibitions as well as other cultural events.
Each center has 5 to 10 volunteers selected, and across the country, DC-Cam has more than 500 Cambodian volunteers to help survivors with their daily routines. Collect oral history and research local community history.
DC-Cam continues to work on increasing the resources available in each center and establishing additional centers in other areas.
