Report: Cambodia's Sustainable Landscape and Ecotourism Sustainability Project will be a new expectation for natural resources and boost local economy
(Phnom Penh): Currently, the Ministry of Environment manages 74 protected areas and 3 biodiversity corridors on a total area of 7.3 million hectares, equivalent to 41% of the country's land area, and has facilitated the establishment of 182 protected area communities. Some communities with potential for eco-tourism are also earning income from providing tourism services for further community livelihood development.
In addition to protecting, conserving natural resources, wildlife and boosting the local economy, with the support of the World Bank, the Ministry of Environment and the Ministry of Rural Development, implement the project: "Sustainability of Cambodia's landscape and eco-tourism" for 6 years (2020-2025 ) The project covers the management of natural resources on an area of more than 3.8 million hectares.
The project will contribute to improving the management of protected areas, promoting the potential for ecotourism and non-timber forest product value chains in the Cardamom and Tonle Sap landscape, and improving the local economy. They avoid the old business, go into the forest, cut down trees, hunt wild animals and so on.
According to Mr. Khieu Borin, Director General of the General Department of Local Affairs of the Ministry of Environment and Director of the "Sustainability of Cambodia's Landscape and Ecotourism Sustainability" project, the Ministry of Environment will do its utmost to establish ecotourism sites within its jurisdiction. Lots to attract tourists.
At the same time, he stressed that for the successful implementation of this project, it is very important to involve the people living in those areas by reducing the use of natural resources such as logging. Traditional clearing for crops and hunting.
Mr. Khin Meng Kheang, Director of the Department of Ecotourism of the Ministry of Environment, said that for the implementation of the project "Sustainability of Cambodia's landscape and ecotourism" only at the Jayavarman Norodom Phnom Kulen National Park, there are five communities that are directly benefiting and Indirectly from this project, while natural resource areas are also benefiting.
In addition to the efforts to implement the project successfully as planned, he said the project also studies the connecting roads that exist in both the community and the forest trail. In addition, they are studying the landscape. To identify areas where there is potential for infrastructure development for the private sector to invest, especially small-scale ecotourism projects.
The director of the Department of Ecotourism of the Ministry of Environment said: "Through this project, we will look at clean water. At the same time, we will support communities to organize community-level ecotourism to further improve their families' livelihoods. ”
Separately, Mr. Phan Phon, President of Prey Thom Anlong Thom Nature Protection Community, confirmed that the people in the community have turned their attention to the tourism sector: Now people have joined together to conserve natural resources as well as wildlife in the temple area to serve the tourism sector. They enjoy the fields, such as raising chickens, ducks, pigs, and especially growing agricultural crops for sale to tourists.
He explained: "After receiving educational guidance from project officials, now a large number of communities have turned to raising chickens, pigs, mice and especially growing agricultural crops for sale to tourists. Sometimes his raising is not. All the needs of tourists.
Through the project "Sustainability of Cambodia's landscape and eco-tourism", he expressed the hope that his area will be further developed, especially the construction of new towers for national and international tourists to visit rare birds. Which are present in the Ramsar Prek Toal area.
Mr. Ton Nay, President of Prek Toal Ecotourism Community, expressed his belief that through the project "Sustainability of Cambodia's landscape and eco-tourism" will help his community to further develop to attract tourists to visit the economy of the community. Progress and so on.
Mr. Nay said that for the Prek Toal ecotourism area, there are 129 species of birds, a total of more than 20,000 birds, regardless of the number of new hatcheries, waiting to receive tourists. The community is also promoting its potential to tourists and participating in the conservation of natural resources and birds in protected areas.
He also appealed to all fishermen not to hunt those birds or trade them for food, because these birds will provide important benefits to the national tourism and serve daily life. Growth through tourists visiting such rare biodiversity.
