(Phnom Penh): To celebrate the 71st anniversary of the Kingdom of Cambodia's accession to UNESCO, the Ministry of Culture and Fine Arts has produced a video celebrating Cambodia's Intangible Cultural Heritage List.
UNESCO is part of the United Nations, which focuses on scientific education. Founded on November 16, 1945, it is on a special mission to maintain world peace and security by promoting cooperation among nations through education, science, culture and communication.
After Angkor was inscribed on the World Heritage List on December 14, 1992, an International Coordinating Committee for the Conservation and Development of the Angkor Historic Site (ICC-Angkor) was established in 1993. The establishment of the ICC-Angkor stems from the conditions set by the World Heritage Committee for the recognition of the Angkor site on the UNESCO World Heritage List.
Three Cambodian temples have been inscribed on the World Heritage List through the UNESCO General Assembly, including: Angkor was inscribed on the World Heritage List in 1992, the Temple of Preah Vihear in 2008, and the Sambor Prei Kuk site in 2017.
Five of Cambodia's intangible cultural properties have also been inscribed on the UNESCO World Heritage List, including: Puppet Theater in 2018, Chapey Dang Veng in 2016, Pulling in 2015, Royal Ballet in 2008. And the Big Skin Shadow Theater in 2008. At the same time, bokator has also been asked by UNESCO to be included in the Intangible World Heritage List.
