Europe agrees to curb state-backed acquisitions of European companies
These new rules suggest a more pro-protection approach against any possible acquisition of a foreign company by a Chinese company.
Last year, the European Commission unveiled proposals for new rules on the acquisition of European companies aimed at curbing what it considers unfair competition from countries such as China.
EU countries and parliamentarians have also agreed to block subsidized foreign companies from participating in public auctions worth more than 250 million euros.
A fine of up to 10% of the company's total revenue could be imposed if the company does not notify the EU of the subsidies they receive. The European Commission will be given the power to investigate subsidies up to five years back after the law goes into effect in mid-2023.
Acquisitions or bidding for contracts made by foreign companies that have received subsidies beyond those levels will lead to investigations and action to counteract the damaging effects. An agreement on these rules was reached after nearly six hours of negotiations.
