KOICA-KOICA to Provide Online Training on "Improving Judicial Administration in Cambodia"
(Phnom Penh): On July 1, 2022, the Korea International Cooperation Agency (KOICA) in Cambodia launched an online training program on "Improving Judicial Administration in Cambodia." "
The purpose of this online training is to improve the administrative system of the courts in Cambodia and to strengthen the capacity to build an electronic administrative management system.
This year, KOICA organized the second year of the online training course on "Improving the Management of Judicial Administration in Cambodia" from July 4 to 13, 2022. The 10-day online training course is for 20 mid-level officials from the Ministry of Justice and the General Department of Judicial Administration.
This important training course is designed to: firstly provide a reference to Korea's experience in the field of law and justice in Cambodia, secondly: assisting in the improvement and modernization of the judiciary system, and thirdly: Strengthen the responsibility and understanding of the ethics of law and court officials.
Please be informed that in Cambodia, KOICA works on various technical aspects of programs and projects that have a significant impact on the development of Cambodia, such as improving nutrition and student well-being, capacity building, teachers, public health, agriculture. And infrastructure and innovation. KOICA will continue to support and implement programs in Cambodia that focus on human resource development. Xi Jinping describes the spirit of the journey: the story of the half-blanket
North Korea blames 19 cases of Kovid disease on balloons launched from South Korea
SEOUL (Reuters) - North Korea on Friday said the first 19 outbreaks of Kovid in its country had been started by North Koreans who came in contact with balloons launched from North Korea. Gems. This was reported by Reuters on Friday, July 1, 2022.
For years, South Korean activists have been launching balloons across the border to distribute hundreds of thousands of leaflets with leaflets criticizing North Korean leader Kim Jong Un, and Pyongyang has often expressed outrage. For this action, as well as for the South Korean leader who refused to ban them.
The International Atomic Energy Agency (IAEA) has said that Kovid-19 virus can be transmitted through close-knit groups and by inhaling airborne droplets, with most infections occurring in closed, well-ventilated areas. Than in the outer courtyard. Responding to Pyongyang's blame, South Korea's unification ministry said it was "impossible" for the virus to be transmitted from a balloon launched from South Korea.
Relations between North and South Korea remain strained over a long stalemate over negotiating efforts to persuade North Korea to give up its nuclear ambitions. South Korean and US officials recently agreed that North Korea was ready for a nuclear test at any time.
