In this circular, the Ministry of Tourism reminded the Capital-Provincial Department of Tourism and the owners or managers of tourism businesses about the implementation of the measures of the Royal Government, the Ministry of Health and Tourism Safety Rules (or Minimum Standards-SOP). These include:
1. To continue to strengthen the implementation of all measures issued by the Ministry of Health and the Tourism Safety Rules of the Ministry of Tourism to prevent the spread of Kovid-19 disease.
3. All tourism service businesses must ensure that all employees are vaccinated with Kovid-19, both local and booster doses, in accordance with the Ministry of Health.
4: Along with measures to prevent the spread of Kovid-19 disease, all tourism service businesses must continue to improve the quality of services, care for the environment - hygiene and maintain the prices of products and services appropriately.
5. The Capital-Provincial Department of Tourism shall continue to cooperate with the Capital-Provincial Administration and local authorities to review and further strengthen the implementation of tourism safety rules (or minimum standard operating procedures-SOP).
6. The Capital-Provincial Department of Tourism shall continue to cooperate with the Capital-Provincial Administration and local authorities to guide all tourism business establishments and require local business managers to notify local authorities of any suspicious cases to guests involved in the disease. Kovid-19 (After scanning the QR Code, block Kovid-19).
7. All businesses must continue to implement all measures in force. In case of failure or improper implementation, the Capital-Provincial Department of Tourism shall request the cooperation of the local authority to stop the business operation and continue the procedure to request the implementation of the applicable laws, especially the Law on Measures to Prevent the Outbreak of Kovid. -19 and other vicious and dangerous diseases.
8. The Capital-Provincial Department of Tourism shall continue to cooperate with the Capital-Provincial Administration and local authorities to inspect the prohibited places to do business, to avoid doing business without asking for a proper permit.
