Vietnam plans to cut WTO tariffs on gasoline to fight inflation
Reuters quoted a statement issued by the Vietnamese government as saying that the measure would also help Vietnam diversify its gasoline imports and reduce its dependence on its major oil suppliers, including South Korea. Gems and neighboring countries in Southeast Asia as well.
The most favorable country tax (MFN) is the standard tariff rate applied in trade between WTO member countries, unless there is a separate trade agreement.
Under the free trade agreement between Vietnam and South Korea and the 10-nation Association of Southeast Asian Nations, the import duty on gasoline is about 8%.
Vietnam, a regional manufacturing hub, reported 7.72% GDP growth in the second quarter of this year, but like many of its neighbors, the country is facing increasing pressure. On inflation due to higher energy and food prices.
The Vietnamese government has said it plans to reduce consumption taxes, especially value-added tax and environmental tax on fuel, but did not provide further details. According to Reuters.
Crude oil prices fall after OPEC + adhere to oil production plan
LONDON (Reuters) - Crude prices fell on Thursday as OPEC + producers vowed to increase output in August, as previously announced, but left markets skeptical. In the future, these countries have not discussed plans for September. According to Reuters.
A group of OPEC + oil producers, including Russia, on Thursday agreed to adhere to its output strategy after a two-day meeting, Reuters reported. These groups no longer discuss policies for September and next month.
Sanctions on Russian oil since its invasion of Ukraine have pushed up energy prices, fueling strong inflation and fears of a recession.
In the United States, oil inventories rose as refiners rose to near full capacity, the highest level in four years, data showed.
However, crude inventories fell 2.8 million barrels in the week ended June 24, far exceeding analysts' estimates in a Reuters poll.
