Cross-border coal smugglers to sell in Vietnam through Men Chey International Gate, Prey Veng Forestry Administration
(Prey Veng): Traders transport unprocessed and unprocessed charcoal from other provinces to be sold in neighboring Vietnam through the Men Chey International Gate, but for professional officials They claim that these are not coal, that is, the original acacia that is banned.
Residents in the area say that day by day, traders rush to transport coal by truck and Samsung or other recycling trucks across the border to be sold in Vietnam through the Men Chey International Border Crossing, Kamchay Mear District, Prey Veng Province. Day and night, without seeing any competent professional officers prevent the crackdown.
Residents said that the coal truck was parked on a road in Romeas Hek district, Svay Rieng province and headed to the Men Chey International Border Crossing, while the authorities were waiting to open the barricade.
At the same time, the traders revealed that the transportation of coal to sell to Vietnamese traders through this international border gate was so smooth that when the coal was shipped out, there were always runners to meet the relevant authorities and officials at the border. After that, the competent authorities and relevant professional officials did not crack down.
According to traders, trucks transporting non-timber forest products processed into cross-border timber for sale in Vietnam through the Men Chey International Border Crossing have been around for months, and such transportation is smooth. They paid the gatekeepers 1 million riel per foot for a car, and only then did the officers wait to open the parade for the coal truck to enter and exit smoothly day and night.
Mr. Ouk Samnang, Director of the Department of Agriculture, Forestry and Fisheries of Prey Veng Province, confirmed that officials do not have a law to allow the export of coal to sell in neighboring countries, but if there is a case, he will have the competent authorities to crack down.
Regarding the case of smuggling coal to be sold in Vietnam through the Men Chey international border crossing, the chief of the Men Chey international border checkpoint could not be reached for comment.
