Following Samdech Techo's order, the Mine Authority of Banteay Meanchey Province, in cooperation with CMAC, will launch a demining operation in Poi Char commune and Ang Trapeang Thmor area next Monday.
(Banteay Meanchey): Samdech Techo Hun Sen, Prime Minister of Cambodia and Chairman of the Mine Authority on July 1, 2022, ordered the Banteay Meanchey Provincial Administration to cooperate with the Mine Authority to end landmines in Poichar commune and Ang Trapeang Thmor area, Phnom Penh. The district, at any cost, to ensure that the area is a mine-free zone.
Samdech Techo's order came after receiving reports of the serious threat of landmines to people in Poi Char commune, as well as in the Ang Trapeang Thmor area, where he released fish fry on the morning of July 1. This 2022.
After receiving Samdech Techo's order, Senior Minister Ly Thuch, Deputy Chief of the Mine Authority, contacted Mr. Ou Rathrey, Governor of Banteay Meanchey Province, and Mr. Heng Ratana, Director General of the Cambodian Mine Action Center, to take immediate action by deploying clearing forces. Monday, July 4, 2022 next week.
"Not only the Trapeang Thmor Reservoir area has been cleared of mines, but the entire Phnom Srok district has been cleared of mines on a land area of 25.1 hectares," he said.
The Senior Minister continued that Samdech Techo not only liberated the country from the genocidal regime and provided peace to the people, Samdech Techo also always cared about the suffering of the people at all times and wanted to see all the people. In both urban and rural areas, everyone lives in safety, protected from landmines and explosive remnants of war.
With this demining work, Samdech Techo Prime Minister of Cambodia also called on the private sector to join the Royal Government in demining, thank you in advance. According to Samdech Techo, some land mines cost only $ 50,000 to $ 100,000, which makes it difficult to seek help from foreign partners, so it requires the government to pay for them, and if the private sector helps, it is really good.
Separately, in Poi Char commune, Phnom Srok district, 1,238,027 square meters were cleared and 185 anti-personnel mines, 3 mines and 2,818 explosive remnants of war were destroyed. The remaining area is 245,673 square meters (equivalent to 6 minefields). Seven victims of landmines and explosive remnants of war, six men, one woman and one boy. One died, four were disabled and two were injured.
