Ms. Sofarina spoke about some of the important projects that DC-Cam has been implementing in Prey Veng province, especially in Kampong Trabek district, including the project on promoting the rights and improving the health status of survivors from Prey Veng province. The Khmer Rouge regime.
Finally, she asked all survivors about age, daily living and health issues. First, she expressed her feelings by seeing that all the survivors were really healthy and strong. But when it comes to health issues, most survivors, especially those over the age of 60, have diabetes, arthritis and high blood pressure.
"... I also learned that the Documentation Center of Cambodia has a plan to protect the health of Khmer Rouge survivors," she said. I admire my uncle who looks old and healthy, but it is through my conversations with my uncle that I realize that most of my aunts have physical illnesses, such as high blood pressure and urinary incontinence. Sweet stomach, dizziness, numbness and so on.
Finally, she urged all survivors to take care of their health and continue to share their experiences with the next generation of children in order to preserve the memory of the history of the Khmer Rouge and contribute to preventing the regime's recurrence in the future.
