China urges US to immediately stop malicious cyber hacking (Video inside) (Beijing) Named the realm of eavesdropping and the realm of secrecy, it urges the country to immediately stop its vicious cyber activities. According to a release by Xinhua.
"China condemns such heinous internet activities by the US government," spokesman Zhao Lijian told a news conference. "We demand that the United States immediately explain and stop such irresponsible behavior."
Zhao pointed out that the United States is a repeat perpetrator of cyber-hacking, saying that by 2020, China had seized more than 42 million malicious computer programs. Citing an earlier report on the website of China's National Computer Network Emergency Response Technology, Zhao said 53.1 percent of foreign programs were tracked back to the United States.
Flights between Siem Reap and Da Nang, Vietnam will resume on July 1, 2022 via the first flight K6840, departing Siem Reap at 15:35, lasting 2 hours. To reach Da Nang province.
During the reopening, the airline will operate three flights a week and will increase to five flights a week from September 2022 and add up to every day in the near future.
Siem Reap and Da Nang are well-known tourist destinations in Indochina that have been plagued by 19 Kovid diseases for two years. The return of Cambodia Angkor Air in cooperation with Vietnam Airlines will help boost demand for flights between the two provinces, especially attracting tourists by creating two countries in one destination.
The flight between Siem Reap and Sihanoukville will start on July 2, with the first flight K6131 departing from Siem Reap at 15:10 to Sihanoukville for one hour.
The resumption of flights is crucial in restoring and promoting economic cooperation and tourism development for the two provinces.
Currently, only Angkor Air provides air transport services for domestic flights, flying daily between Phnom Penh and Siem Reap since February 2022.
For flights between Siem Reap and Sihanoukville, there are three flights a week.
Mr. Leav Minhchung said that the company will increase the number of flights for these two routes, especially to increase daily flights between Siem Reap and Sihanoukville from August 2022.
Russia's invasion of Ukraine has raised concerns about oil supplies, pushing prices to record highs this year.
Following the EU ban on oil imports from Russia due to the invasion, OPEC +, at its last meeting in early June, agreed to launch the engine more widely than expected.
The 13 members of the Organization of the Petroleum Exporting Countries, led by Saudi Arabia and Russia, and its 10 partners, have cut production significantly by 2020 due to the global Kovid 19 pandemic.
From May last year, they began to gradually increase production again. In recent months, the United States and other major oil consumers have increased proposals to increase oil output.
But some OPEC + members have been failing to meet their output quotas, while Iran and Venezuela, and now Russia, are also blocked by Western sanctions.
