Minister of Commerce gives a lecture on "Potentials and Benefits of a Free Trade Agreement Entering into Force" at the Royal School of Administration
(Phnom Penh): Mr. Pan Sosak, Minister of Commerce, on the afternoon of June 30, 2022, addressed a panel discussion on "Potentials and Benefits of the Free Trade Agreement in force" for senior officials. Senior 12th Generation and all trainees in 2022 of the Royal School of Administration at the Royal School of Administration.
A free trade agreement (FTA) is a mutual tax exemption agreement between one country and another on an agreed product. The FTA is not only a product tax exemption agreement, but covers many areas, including services, investment, trade facilitation, and the removal of non-tariff barriers.
The Regional Comprehensive Trade Partnership Agreement (RCEP) is attended by 15 countries, including the 10 ASEAN member states, and five other free trade partners - Australia, China, Japan, South Korea and New Zealand, which were first established in 2012 in Phnom Penh. The RCEP is the largest trade agreement in the world, with an estimated population of 2.2 billion, or 30 percent of the world's population, and 28 percent of world trade.
During the speech, the Minister of Commerce confirmed that Cambodia also plans to establish new free trade agreements with a number of key trading partners, including the European Union, Asia, India, Russia, Japan and Mongolia.
