The Bank of Japan will keep its monetary policy in place as the economy is less affected by global inflation. According to a report by Reuters.
Japan's inflation rose to 2.1 percent for the second straight month in May, Reuters reported, citing central bank Governor Haruhiko Kuroda as saying at a seminar. Is driven almost entirely by rising energy prices.
While inflation could remain at around 2 percent in about a year, it is likely to fall to around 1 percent in the next fiscal year, which begins in April 2023, Kuroda said.
"Unlike other economies, the Japanese economy is not much affected by global inflation trends, so monetary policy will continue to be stimulating," he said.
Kuroda said at a seminar in Basel on Sunday that as a result of the 15-year deflation crisis that lasted until 2013, companies in Japan were very cautious about raising commodity prices. Wages.
"The economy has recovered and the company has recorded high profits," he said. The job market is pretty cramped. "But wages have not risen much and commodity prices have not risen much."
Rising global commodity prices and a weaker yen pushing up raw material import costs pushed Japan's core inflation above the central bank's 2 percent target.
But Kuroda has repeatedly stressed the need to keep interest rates as low as possible until inflation is further driven by strong demand, making the Bank of Japan one of the world's central banks. Raised interest rates to fight rising inflation According to Reuters.
Chinese PM says Chinese economy is recovering but foundations are not strong
Reuters quoted Chinese state media as saying that Li would work to push the economy back to normal and reduce unemployment as soon as possible. Go.
"Currently, the implementation of the policy package for economic stability is dynamic and effective," Li was quoted as saying. "The economy has fully recovered, but the fundamentals are not yet strong." "The task of maintaining job stability remains difficult," he added.
China's economy showed signs of recovery in May after a slump in April as industrial production rebounded but consumption remained weak and posed a challenge for policymakers. When there is a series of interruptions due to measures to prevent Kovid-19.
China's unemployment rate fell to 5.9 percent in May from 6.1 percent in April, but remained higher than the 2022 target, according to a survey. Of the Chinese government with a rate below 5.5 percent.
In particular, the unemployment rate surveyed in 31 major cities rose to a record 6.9 percent. Some economists expect jobs to get worse before they improve, with the largest number of graduates entering the summer job market.
Li vowed to achieve reasonable economic growth in the second quarter, although some private sector economists expect a slowdown in the second quarter of this year compared to the same period last year. The economy grew 4.8 percent in the first quarter of this year. According to Reuters.
