Cambodia celebrates World Food Security Day to highlight the need to prioritize food security at all stages of the food chain
(Phnom Penh): Like HIV or malaria, foodborne illness is one of the world's most serious public health challenges. More than 200 different diseases are directly related to eating foods that contain bacteria, viruses, parasites and chemicals.
According to the World Health Organization (WHO), food-borne illnesses cause an estimated 42,000 deaths worldwide each year, especially among children under the age of five and low-income people. Low. Africa and Southeast Asia have the highest incidence and death rates. This reflects the urgent need to ensure food security.
Foodborne illness can cause short-term symptoms such as nausea and diarrhea (commonly called food poisoning), but it can also lead to long-term illnesses such as cancer, kidney disease, liver problems, encephalopathy and neurological disorders. Nerves. These diseases can be severe in children, pregnant women and the elderly, or those with weakened immune systems.
Children who have suffered from foodborne illness may experience serious complications, such as delayed physical and mental development, which will permanently affect their quality of life. But the good news is that foodborne illness is a preventable disease.
The celebration aims to raise public awareness of food safety issues and to increase awareness of the importance of food safety to ensure consumer protection and the development of the food industry in Cambodia.
The celebration of World Food Security Day in Cambodia highlights the need to strengthen efforts to ensure food safety for consumers and integrate food safety into the public agenda to reduce foodborne illness. And facilitate business opportunities for Cambodia.
The World Food Security Day celebration was attended by about 250 participants from all stages of the food chain, including policy makers, national and sub-national technical officials, representatives from the private sector, retailers, farmers, professors, university students and more. Media.
Food security is a complex challenge in Cambodia. The Royal Government of Cambodia has worked hard and is strongly committed to addressing food security issues.
Deputy Prime Minister Yim Chhay Ly stressed that "Food security, food security and food systems are multi-sectoral issues that require good cooperation, coordination, intervention and investment in agriculture, health, education, water and sanitation, water resources management, social protection and Relevant sectors.
He added that the cooperation of all stakeholders, including state institutions, development partners, civil society, the private sector, the youth community and the media to implement the above-mentioned sectoral and multi-sectoral policies and strategies is essential to address the complexities of the root causes of nutrition problems. All forms.
Ms. Rebekah Bell added that FAO is ready to work with the Royal Government of Cambodia, development partners, civil society organizations and the private sector to contribute to the implementation of the Food Safety Act, which has been approved in support of the necessary regulations. Discuss food safety policies, especially the analysis of food control systems in Cambodia this year.
An analysis of the food control system is very important as this is a collection of evidence, a detailed analysis of that evidence to prepare specific recommendations and be able to be implemented by all stakeholders to work together to address food security effectively.
