As the Special Envoy of the ASEAN Chair on Myanmar, Deputy Prime Minister Prak Sokhon, Minister of Foreign Affairs and International Cooperation, led a delegation on a second five-day working visit to the Republic of the Union of Myanmar. The visit will take place from June 29 to July 3, 2022.
Accompanying the Special Envoy were Mr. Ek Pub Phanthavong, Deputy Secretary-General of ASEAN in charge of the ASEAN Socio-Cultural Community and a number of senior government officials, as well as staff of the ASEAN Secretariat.
Speaking to reporters before leaving his homeland, Prak Sokhon said that under the ASEAN chairmanship, Cambodia was working to find a solution in Myanmar in order to reach a national dialogue aimed at ending the conflict.
According to Prak Sokhon, based on Cambodia's experience in how to resolve disputes, Myanmar can draw on the experiences and lessons that Cambodia has gone through. "We are not forcing him to follow in our footsteps, but what he sees as useful for the process of progress towards peace, for long-term stable national unity, he can take," he said. All that.
The Deputy Prime Minister added that this second visit is also a demonstration of Cambodia's efforts as his special envoy and as Cambodia's 2022 ASEAN chair. Cambodia hopes to take another step in addressing humanitarian aid and also take another step in building trust among all stakeholders.
Ahead of his second visit, Deputy Prime Minister Prak Sokhon sent a letter to the Council of State (SAC) of the Union of Myanmar expressing "grave concern of ASEAN colleagues" over the report on the transfer of Aung San Suu Kyi from her residence. He was imprisoned in Nay Pyi Taw and urged the State Administrative Council to facilitate her repatriation, where she was initially detained.
Please be informed that Deputy Prime Minister Prak Sokhon paid his first working visit to Myanmar as the Special Envoy of the ASEAN Chair for 3 days from 21 to 23 March 2022 at the invitation of The State Administrative Council of the Republic of the Union of Myanmar after the country's leader, Min Aung Lan, agreed to some compromises.
