It should be noted that the two houses above are 4 meters × 5 meters made of zinc and 3 toilets are 2 meters × 2 meters made of brick and have additional gifts, groceries and some budget. Angkor Hospital for Children launches 'patient contribution' to sustain operations as funding sources dwindle
(Siem Reap): Angkor Hospital for Children, which has been a free humanitarian hospital, has now decided to implement the "Patient Donation" from July 7, 2022 in order to maintain the operation of the hospital. That costs more.
Hospital costs have risen sharply in recent years as funding sources to support the hospital's operations have struggled, especially during the Kovid 19 outbreak.
In a press release obtained by Fresh News on June 29, 2022, the humanitarian hospital announced that it will start implementing the payment of "patient contributions" on hospital health services from July 7, 2022. This 2022 onwards. Implement this "patient contribution" payment to ensure the sustainability of the mission of providing medical examination and care services to patients.
In a statement, the hospital stated that for those who can afford to pay, the hospital is required to pay for outpatient and outpatient services. For outpatient services, the fee is 20,000 riel or $ 5 per visit, and the hospital fee is 20,000 riel or $ 5 per night.
Dr. Nguon Chanpheaktra, Director of Angkor Hospital for Children, emphasized that "this payment will not affect the health services that patients receive."
"The hospital will continue to provide the highest quality and compassionate health services through the same caring care for all children in need of our services," he said. "Regardless of the family's living conditions."
For many years, Angkor Hospital for Children has relied on local and international charitable donations. As foreign aid has declined, staff in the country have begun to seek additional charitable assistance in the country, but hospitals still face some obstacles in seeking total funding to run the hospital year after year.
Dr. Nguon Chanpheaktra said that patient donations are a way to encourage all Cambodians to help each other, with those who are able to help those who cannot. Through this partnership, Angkor Children's Hospital will continue to open its doors to treat patients in the long run and continue to rescue and improve the health care system for Cambodian children.
Angkor Hospital for Children continues to provide free care to patients with poor card or health equity fund card and patients with poverty proof by Angkor Hospital Children's Social Work Team. Whether patients who can or cannot afford to pay a contribution, Angkor Hospital for Children continues to provide the same quality and compassionate medical care and care to children.
Angkor Hospital for Children still needs donations from philanthropists to completely reduce the cost of health care fees, with a large portion being the contribution of philanthropists through donations.
"We hope that the parents of all patients who can afford to contribute will join us to support the further development of high quality, specialized and compassionate health care services for all Cambodian children." This was confirmed by Mr. Chhim Sovong, Deputy Director of Finance Office of Angkor Hospital for Children.
