Malaysia to spend $ 18 billion on anti-inflation subsidies Video inside Malaysia Malaysia is expected to spend $ 77.3 billion Ringgit ($ 17.6 billion) in subsidies and cash aid this year, the largest amount in history to help offset the effects of rising prices. According to Reuters.
Commodity prices have soared in Malaysia in recent months due to disruption of the supply chain, labor shortages and the effects of the war in Ukraine.
Food inflation rose to 5.2 percent in May from May last year, the highest level since November 2011, Reuters reported, citing government data released last week.
Malaysia is expected to spend 51 billion ringgit on consumer subsidies, including for fuel, electricity and food, Malaysian Finance Minister Tengku Zafrul Aziz said in a statement. Assume that the market price of the commodity remains at the current level.
He said the government would also release 11.7 billion ringgit in cash aid and 14.6 billion ringgit for other subsidies.
Malaysia said last week it would release nearly $ 400 million this month to help families facing rising food and living costs.
