Teaching online is a good way, but can not be used to teach all subjects.
(Phnom Penh): In the age of advanced technology, the online system has played an important role in various fields, including education, the online system was also used, especially during the Kovid 19 disease invaded the world.
In Cambodia, the use of online systems for education has also been observed to increase significantly, even when Kovid 19 has recovered and the country has been reopened.
However, Mr. Kouch Mengly, an expert in the field of education, said that online teaching is a good way, but it can not be used to teach all subjects.
Mr. Kouch Mengly continued that although soft skills can be taught online, they are required to come to class, that is, they can not teach this skill 100 percent using the online system. Teaching only online completely can make you lazy and less knowledgeable.
He added that he allowed his educational institution to use this program for English only because it is in soft skills. He added that if the online system is used to teach 20 or more students, the learners will not gain knowledge.
The expert in the field of education also said that the online system is very important to promote the education sector, but if it is misused, it will weaken the sector.
Ros Sovacha, spokesman for the Ministry of Education, Youth and Sports, said that online learning and teaching can be both a substitute for learning and teaching in the classroom, depending on the context. Context is an important factor in explaining the relationship between learning and teaching online and learning and teaching in the classroom.
For general education level, online learning and teaching is used to complement the value of learning and teaching in the classroom, which should not be inseparable depending on the priorities of each country.
For postgraduate and postgraduate students, as well as online training courses for some majors, are used to add value to live learning, as at secondary level. For some subjects, online learning can be done 100% depending on the socio-economic context of each country in providing educational services.
