Letter from readers: "Authorities should take hot action against former NagaWorld employees"
It sounds ridiculous and embarrassing. It is ridiculous that this group took money in exchange for rights, that is, they took money from foreigners through other mechanisms in exchange for the right to protest illegally, disrupt public order and cause anarchy in the capital. For almost a year now.
The shameful thing is that this group is using their rights illegally by forcing employers to accept them to work when they are not needed. In other words, the Chhim Sithor group could not find a new job because they did not know how to do anything other than the skills of illegal demonstrations, causing chaos in the company and in public places.
So, in our world, is there any employer who hires this type of employee to work for his company? That is why they want to exercise their right to force themselves to work, so-called labor rights.
This is not a labor right, but a compulsion to get a job. Such illegal use of rights, even demonstrations to the point of gray hair, no one accepts to work. Not to mention that NagaWorld, whether companies all over Cambodia, whether small or large, are not accepted, because if this type of person is hired to work, it is equivalent to using rubber band to pinch the armpit or like a bomb to set the time of the explosion. To put in the company only.
From the beginning until now, those who have watched this event must have clearly understood the purpose and political agenda of this small group of former NagaWorld employees.
Their goal is not to demand labor rights, but to be the union president of NagaWorld to continue to shake up the company, which employs thousands of workers.
People who talk about rights and understand their rights need to understand their own rights and the rights of others. Speaking of work, it is the rights of workers and the rights of employers.
When it comes to the rights of ordinary people, rioting on public streets or blocking public roads is a violation of the rights of others.
Therefore, a small group of former NagaWorld employees led by political activist Chhim Sithor does not know the rights and does not respect the rights of others.
When they violate the rights of others, their rights will one day be over. So far, I have seen the authorities seem too tolerant and favoring the anarchists. Hot measures should also be taken to maintain social order and protect the general human rights that this group is violating, including the rights of the authorities, which have caused injuries and loss of property.
So how long should this small group of people be allowed to cause anarchy and violate the rights of others?
By: A law student at a university in Phnom Penh, Mr. Soeng Samnang, led a member of the Cambodian People's Party in Kampong Leng district to celebrate the 71st birthday of the Cambodian People's Party
