Phnom Penh Capital Hall Tells NagaWorld Former Employees and Employees to Stop Using Public Roads for Illegal Gatherings or Parades
(Phnom Penh): Phnom Penh Capital Administration issued a press release that on the afternoon of June 27, 2022, a group of more than 70 former employees and employees of NagaWorld continued to gather illegally in the center of the Great Samdech Sothearos Street, walking on the road from in front of Aeon Mall 1 to Neak Banh Tuek traffic light.
In the past, the Phnom Penh Municipal Administration has issued several notices not to allow protesters, former employees and employees of NagaWorld, to gather illegally on public streets, sidewalks, public parks and public places. Others have also used a series of educational measures against protesters to stop further illegal activities.
The Phnom Penh Municipal Administration would like to re-instruct the protesters in case they wish to hold a public opinion, to follow the procedures set out in the Law on Peaceful Demonstrations by notifying the Phnom Penh Municipal Authority in writing and Follow the instructions and conduct a demonstration at Freedom Park.
(Phnom Penh): The Khmer-Soviet Friendship Hospital and the Heart Association of the Republic of Korea on June 27, 2022 signed a Memorandum of Understanding on the training of human resources in the field of health, especially in the field of heart disease.
The MoU signing ceremony was presided over by Prof. Chan Vichet, Deputy Director of the Khmer-Soviet Friendship Hospital, and a team of doctors, including Professor Park Hyun-iem, Cardiologist, Director of the Cardiovascular Intervention Center of Severon Hospital, Republic of Korea. And Professor Chon Wang, Cardiologist, Head of the Rhythm Department at River Health Center, USA.
On that occasion, Professor Park Hee Nam said, "The signing of this memorandum to train the knowledge of cardiology to Cambodian doctors. In the past, we have also trained this skill to the doctors of the Khmer-Soviet Friendship Hospital, along with providing equipment. "Therefore, in signing this memorandum, I expect to help develop the heart disease to a special standard, strengthen the relationship between the two countries to a better level."
At the same time, Professor Chan Vichet said, "In the past, our Cambodian students, who are doctors of the Khmer-Soviet Friendship Hospital, went abroad to study directly with the Rhythm Association of the Republic of Korea to develop cardiology." In Cambodia, to the standard level for the people, today we signed a memorandum together.
According to Dr. Lim Sivuth, Director of the Cardiovascular Department of the Khmer-Soviet Friendship Department, from year to year, the number of patients who come to the hospital for cardiology. Khmer-Soviet friendship grew rapidly from middle age to old age, most of which was old age.
"A few years ago, our hospital only treated normal heart disease, but now we have a lot of confidence, including cardiac implants," he said. But after signing a memorandum of understanding with the Heart Association of the Republic of Korea, we will have more heart rate treatment.
On that occasion, the doctor pointed out that heart disease is a chronic disease, but also a disease that poses an immediate danger to life without knowing it. Therefore, to prevent better than cure is to have a proper diet, not to be overweight, exercise regularly and go for regular check-ups.
