(Preah Sihanouk): The Preah Sihanouk Provincial Administration and the Provincial Department of Agriculture, Forestry and Fisheries have expressed their commitment to protect the flooded forest and requested legal action against those who cut down the flooded forest for personal use.
This commitment was made during the re-planting ceremony of 7,000 mangrove trees on the flooded forest area of more than 7 hectares that has been destroyed in the past in Champour Khmao village, Tuol Toteung commune, Prey Nob district, Preah Sihanouk province on June 25, 2022
Nen Chamroeun, director of the Sihanoukville Provincial Department of Agriculture, Forestry and Fisheries, said that by 2021, authorities had planted 5,500 mangrove trees and planted 4,000 mangrove trees on 4.50 hectares of land. Geography: Prey Nob District and Koh Rong City, Preah Sihanouk Province.
He said that in the first six months of 2022, the authorities planted 60,500 mangrove trees and planted 3,600 mangrove trees on 5 hectares of land in Prey Nob district and Koh Rong city of Sihanoukville province.
In fact, today, the Kampong Som Fisheries Administration District of Preah Sihanouk Provincial Department of Agriculture, Forestry and Fisheries replanted another 7,000 mangrove trees on 7 hectares of land that had been destroyed in the past. Some crooks have been stealing and occupying their own land, and the Department of Agriculture has urged the Fisheries Administration to continue planting and replanting mangrove seedlings.
Mr. Nen Chamroeun further stated that the Department of Agriculture of Preah Sihanouk Province also strives to prevent and prevent the crime of destroying mangrove forests in order to protect the mangrove forest for the public interest.
Mr. Kong Vitanak, Deputy Governor of Preah Sihanouk Province, noted that the mangrove forest is very important to be protected because it is a habitat for fish, spawning, natural resources that attract tourists, help improve the lives of poor farmers in the area.
The Deputy Governor requested the Fisheries Administration officials and all stakeholders, especially the local authorities of villages / communes, to provide support and cooperation to maintain conservation and to take legal action against those who cut down the forest. Submerged for personal use.
The Lower Mekong River maintains an ecosystem of the world's largest giant trout.
At this point, he said, the lower Mekong River still maintains an excellent ecosystem, which is the safest place for the largest giant trout in the world.
Thus, Cambodia found the world's largest giant trout, confirming that China's construction of dams did not affect the ecology of the Lower Mekong.
The giant trout is a species of fish that likes to live in clean and unpolluted rivers. Among the largest species of freshwater fish in the world, a giant trout is found in the Lower Mekong countries. Recently, a giant trout weighing about 300 kg was found. In Stung Treng province, which is the Mekong River area of Cambodia.
From its origins in the western highlands of China to the Mekong Delta, bordering the South China Sea, the Mekong River is more than 4,000 km long and flows through Myanmar, Laos, Thailand, Cambodia and Vietnam. The river supports the livelihoods of more than 60 million people living along this waterway.
The discovery of the giant trout proves that these allegations are baseless and negative. In contrast, the Lower Mekong River is still free-flowing, of good quality, and still maintains a good ecosystem, resulting in the presence of giant trout and other rare large species of fish.
As an Upper Mekong country, China has always paid close attention to the concerns and needs of the Lower Mekong countries and has maintained close ties and is committed to implementing water resources cooperation with relevant parties of the Lower Mekong countries. Such as sharing hydrological information, flood management and drought relief.
