Thailand, UK vow to boost bilateral trade to $ 7 billion
LONDON (Reuters) - Thailand and the United Kingdom aim to boost bilateral trade to $ 7 billion as soon as possible after trade between the two countries plummeted. Strongly in the last 5 years. According to the Bangkok Post.
The Bangkok Post quoted Thai Commerce Minister Jurin Laksanawisit as saying after the first joint Thai-British Economic and Trade Committee meeting in London on Tuesday. An important new opportunity to boost bilateral trade, including addressing trade barriers affecting business activities in both countries.
According to Jurin, participants discussed potential partnerships in six areas, including digital, agriculture, food and beverage, healthcare, financial services, as well as trade and investment promotion activities.
He said the two sides agreed to expedite the partnership in these six areas as soon as a meeting of senior officials was scheduled to hold joint meetings between the two sides and plan action to increase trade volume. Bilateral programs reach $ 7 billion.
The partnership will launch another ambitious work program to boost trade in a range of areas, which will include the implementation of trade policy cooperation, trade barriers and the promotion of business and trade activities.
