Before the collapse of the Soviet Union, Kaliningrad was not an issue for Europe, as at that time both Poland and Lithuania, as well as two other Baltic countries, Estonia and Latvia, were already in the Soviet Union with Russia. But after the collapse of the Soviet Union in 1991, Lithuania, Poland, Estonia and Latvia became members of NATO and the European Union, which separated Kaliningrad from Russia, with Poland and Belarus, Russia's allies in the middle.
