Bangladesh buys wheat from Russia after India suspends exports (Video inside) (Dhaka): Bangladesh is trying to secure a supply of wheat from Russia in a government-to-government agreement after India Its largest supplier banned the export of the grain last month to keep domestic prices low. According to Reuters.
A supply deal with Russia, the world's largest wheat exporter, could help Dhaka meet its demand at below-market prices, Reuters reported, citing Bangladeshi government officials. Global prices have risen.
A senior official from the Ministry of Food said Bangladesh was holding an online meeting with Russia on Thursday to finalize the agreement. "First, we will buy at least 200,000 tons of wheat from Russia," the official said on condition of anonymity.
The Bangladesh Food Ministry did not immediately respond to a request for comment from Reuters.
Bangladesh imports about 7 million tonnes of wheat, and last year more than two-thirds of its imports came from India. After a ban on exports by India, Bangladesh tried to find supplies through international auctions but canceled due to high prices.
A Bangladeshi-based trader working for a world-class trading company told Reuters that Bangladesh paid less than $ 400 a tonne for Indian wheat, but after India banned exports. Other wheat suppliers began to raise prices above $ 460, pushing up domestic prices in Bangladesh.
The Bangladeshi government is struggling with rising commodity prices, with inflation hitting an eight-year high in May as the country's wheat stocks fell to a three-year low. 166,000 tons.
