(Kratie): Mr. Net Pheaktra, Secretary of State and Spokesman of the Ministry of Environment, said that Cambodian officials and relevant organizations have been compiling and preparing documents to apply for the protection of dolphin canyon in Prek Kampi from Kratie to the province. Stung Treng to be inscribed on the UNESCO World Heritage List, in line with the proposal for the Phnom Tbeng Protected Area in Preah Vihear Province.
At the same time, he revealed that while the other three areas, including: Prek Prosop Wildlife Sanctuary in Kratie Province, Cardamom Mountain National Park and Phnom Nam Leav, Mondulkiri Province have not yet been considered.
Mr. Neth Pheaktra made the remarks during a press conference on Trap Zero in Kratie province last week with the participation of the provincial administration, the provincial environment department and partner organizations.
The spokesman stated that in order for the protected area of the dolphin canyon in Prek Kampi in Kratie province to be worthy of the value of the World Heritage Site, all citizens must participate in the protection and conservation and prevent fishing. Illegal, especially the use of nets and all kinds of electric devices in the dolphin canyon, which caused the death of Mekong River dolphins.
Mr. Neth Pheaktra added: “The Mekong River Dolphin Area from Kratie to Stung Treng is an area that has been identified on two priority lists, in addition to the Phnom Tbeng Natural Heritage Area and the Dolphin Conservation Area. "Around the Mekong River, which is home to two dolphin sanctuaries, we are looking at global values for the preparation of documents for UNESCO."
The Secretary of State of the Ministry of Environment stated that the Prek Kampi Dolphin Canyon Conservation Area is a natural tourism area rich in diverse resources, including: dolphins, rare fish species such as carp, giant trout, rare birds such as river trout, giant vultures, vultures, frog heads, forest areas. Floods and islands that provide well-being for deer, reindeer and more than 800 other species of fish.
The senior official expressed hope that the proposed inclusion of Cambodia's natural areas in the World Natural Heritage List has many benefits, such as making the world more interested in these areas and the world will attract visitors. Tourists from around the world will visit these areas more and more, which is an important part of increasing the income of local people.
In addition, Mr. Neth Pheaktra said that the Royal Government, through the Ministry of Environment, has promoted the protection of the ecological role in the protected area of dolphin canyons from Prek Kampi, Kratie to the border of Stung Treng for the benefit of all generations. Values for natural resources, entertainment, education and scientific research.
He added: "The Prek Kampi Dolphin Canyon should be a priority list, as well as a proposal to include the Phnom Tbeng Protected Area on the World Heritage List, as it has rare and endangered species on it. "The world aims to increase the protection and conservation of these areas for economic benefit and to save the remaining endangered species."
It should be noted that the Royal Government of Cambodia has established the Mekong River Dolphin Management and Protection Area, which stretches along the northern Mekong River for 180 km from the Cambodia-Laos border line at Lak Khon, Nimit Anlong Chheuteal, Anlong Morakot waterfalls in Stung Treng Province. To the head of His Island in Kratie City, Kratie Province.
Mr. Neth Pheaktra stated that Prek Kampi Canyon is an important habitat for Mekong River dolphins and has potential for eco-tourism, which brings a source of income to the community there. He estimated that the number of Mekong freshwater dolphins could be as high as 89, which is higher than Burma and Indonesia. It is estimated that there are only about 60 dolphins.
A study by the Global Fund for Nature (WWF) shows that the number of Cambodian Mekong dolphins has declined significantly, compared to about 200 in 1997 and 127 in 2005. The decline is due to threats posed by fishermen, disruption of their habitats and food sources, and the inclusion of dam projects across the upper Mekong.
The Phnom Tbeng Natural Heritage Area was established on September 13, 2016 by the Royal Government of Cambodia at the request of the Minister of Environment. Sangkum Thmey, Kulen, Rovieng and Preah Vihear districts of Preah Vihear province.
As for the area's natural resource potential, the area remains rich in dense forests, such as dense and semi-dense forests, for wildlife conservation, ecosystem services, and ensuring the supply of fresh water to communities around the area. Ministry of Tourism organizes visit package to Techo Heritage National Stadium in connection with other potential destinations around
