Cambodia Tells ASEAN Members It Wants to See the South China Sea as a Sea of Peace, Stability, Cooperation and Prosperity (Video inside) (Phnom Penh): Samdech Pich Sena Tea Banh, Deputy Prime Minister and Minister of National Defense and Chairman of the ASEAN Defense Ministers' Meeting As the 16th ASEAN Chair, Cambodia wishes to see the South China Sea as a sea of peace, stability, cooperation and prosperity.
This statement was made by Samdech Pich Sena Tea Banh at the 16th ASEAN Defense Ministers' Meeting (16th ADMM) and related meetings in 2022, which Cambodia is hosting this morning, June 22, 2022. At the office of the Ministry of National Defense.
At the same time, Samdech Pich Sena Tea Banh said that the regional maritime context has developed significantly and these developments have also caused many challenges related to maritime security. He stressed the importance of promoting practical maritime cooperation that we must continue to work together.
The President of the 16th ASEAN Defense Ministers' Meeting stated: "On the issue of the South China Sea, Cambodia wishes to see this sea as a sea of peace, stability, cooperation and prosperity. We will continue our commitment to working together between ASEAN and China to promote the consistent implementation of the DOC and expedite the negotiation of the COC Code of Conduct, which is effective and in line with international law, including UNCLOS 1982.
At the same time, Samdech Picheay Sena once again called for patience and avoidance of the use of force in solving problems.
According to Samdech Tea Sena Banh, today ASEAN needs multilateral trends such as ASEAN mechanisms and solidarity between countries to address these challenges together. Cambodia, as chair of ADMM 2022, does not want to see any divisions and is highly committed to strengthening solidarity among ASEAN members and with dialogue partners to jointly address environmental challenges.
The South China Sea issue has become a long-running territorial dispute between China and four ASEAN countries, including Indonesia, Malaysia, the Philippines and Vietnam. The conflict began to escalate as China began building artificial islands in the South China Sea, as ASEAN and the international community called on all conflicting countries to exercise restraint to find a peaceful solution.
In 2002, as Cambodia chaired ASEAN for the first time, the ASEAN-China Code of Conduct for the South China Sea (DOC) was issued. Countries are also working to push for a declaration of a code of conduct on the South China Sea (COC) that is acceptable to end conflicts in the South China Sea.
In December 2021, the head of the Royal Government of Cambodia announced in advance to the international community not to blame Cambodia if the Declaration on the Code of Conduct on the South China Sea (COC) could not be issued in 2022 as it was. Cambodia chaired ASEAN in 2012. Samdech revealed that the reason behind the inability to issue the COC statement at that time was forcing Cambodia to be a judge on the sovereignty of the South China Sea.
