In relations between the two countries after the French colonial period, two important historical events took place to remember: Cambodia's sacrifice in the cause of Vietnam's unification and Vietnam's sacrifice in contributing to the liberation of the Cambodian people from the genocidal regime. Pol Pot.
The war between North Vietnam, backed by the communist bloc, and South Vietnam, aided by US troops, erupted. This war is commonly referred to as the "Vietnam War", but in fact it did not occur only in Vietnam, but also spread to parts of Cambodia and Laos. That is why it is called the "Second Indochina War."
Because of the sacrifices made to help Vietnam, as mentioned above, the United States had an excuse to drop bombs on Cambodia, which caused the deaths and injuries of tens of thousands of Cambodians and dragged Cambodia down. In the Great Crisis and Disaster almost disappeared the Khmer territory and race.
In April 1975, with the sacrifice and wholehearted support of Cambodia, Vietnam conquered the United States and reunited its territory, while Cambodia fell into the genocidal regime of Democratic Kampuchea or the Khmer Rouge.
The mutual sacrifice between Cambodia and Vietnam must be recorded and remembered by both nations. In Cambodia, the merits of Vietnam's liberation from the genocidal regime are commemorated every year, and Cambodian-Vietnamese memorials are allowed to be built in almost every provincial capital of Cambodia.
The Vietnamese Prime Minister also expressed his special gratitude to the Royal Government for pursuing Cambodia's positive neutral policy, recognizing the South Vietnamese National Liberation Front, wholeheartedly contributing to the Vietnamese revolution in building strength at the grassroots level. And a transport route to the battlefield of South Vietnam in those difficult circumstances.
Since 1979, Cambodia and Vietnam have signed agreements to help each other in almost all areas of defense, economy, trade, culture, education, social affairs and health. In the field of defense, Vietnam has provided military equipment to Cambodia. Many students and cadres of the People's Republic of Cambodia and the State of Cambodia were sent to study in Vietnam and other socialist countries.
At the same time, after the day of liberation on January 7, 1979, Cambodia experienced all kinds of shortages. Vietnam, as well as some friendly socialist countries, especially the Soviet Union, provided assistance to Cambodia in the form of food, medicine and groceries. Many others.
The period from 1979 to 1989 was a difficult period for Cambodia to fall into the flames of civil war. The Government of the People's Republic of Cambodia and the State of Cambodia, on the one hand, must prevent the return of Democratic Kampuchea and on the other hand must restore and rebuild the nation. So Vietnamese aid was very important at that time.
Changes in the international political situation in the late 1980s brought Cambodian parties to the conflict back to the negotiating table for peace. Peace talks in Cambodia departed from the historic meeting between Samdech Norodom Sihanouk and Samdech Techo Hun Sen on December 2, 1987 in Fer-en-Tardenois, France. Negotiations took place in large numbers across the globe, including France, Thailand, Indonesia, North Korea, China, Japan and New York.
Finally, on October 23, 1991, in Paris, France, a peace agreement was signed by the four Cambodian parties, with the participation of more than twenty countries and 18 signatories, including Vietnam. . The agreement led to an election in 1993 organized by the United Nations.
Since the early 1990s, relations between Cambodia and Vietnam have entered a new political turning point, in which Vietnam maintained a communist political regime, while Cambodia adopted a liberal, multi-party democracy and a constitutional monarchy. Re-create.
In the face of internal and external context, bilateral relations between the two countries are developing in many fields. Cambodia and Vietnam agree that strengthening and promoting friendly relations and cooperation between the two countries is of great importance to both nations. Overall, the bilateral foreign policy of Cambodia and Vietnam to each other highlights the strength of relations between the two countries.
In expressing their position on each other, the leaders of the two countries have always expressed their commitment to strengthen, expand and enhance the relationship between the two countries to a new level, which brings real benefits. Certainly for the people and the two countries and will contribute positively to peace, stability, cooperation and prosperity in the region and the world.
