BANGKOK: Global food shortages are expected to increase during the third and fourth quarters of this year, and the food shortages are likely to benefit exporters, according to the Federation of Thai Industries (FTI). Thai food. According to the Bangkok Post.
Purchasing food stocks is likely to happen inevitably after crop yields fall due to a shortage of fertilizers and animal feed during the ongoing Russian-Ukrainian war. This conflict is the cause of rising prices for some fertilizers and raw materials.
Russia is a major producer and exporter of steel and a major producer of wheat and corn for livestock, while Ukraine is a major exporter of wheat.
The FTI believes the Thai food industry will benefit from the situation as orders rise amid growing concerns over food shortages.
"Thai food factories have prepared raw materials for food processing to meet the growing demand in the war-affected world food market," he said.
The FTI added that it was not too concerned about the shortage of raw materials in Thailand, which is rich in agricultural products.
