Ministry of Environment and Law Enforcement Commit to Ending Traps and Illegal Wildlife Trafficking Crisis in Cambodia
(Kratie): The end of the crisis of trapping and illegal wildlife trade must start from the activities of local people and the strict implementation of the law by local authorities.
Relevant Royal Government Institutions and international partners in the field of environment have jointly announced the launch of the Zero Trap Campaign in the Cambodian Protected Areas and are committed to taking the necessary steps to end the trap and wildlife trade crisis. Law in Cambodia.
"Based on the initial results, I would like to dedicate the spirit and values of the campaign to be remembered and nurtured by the Cambodian people and all stakeholders to encourage further joint action to ensure "There are no traps in the Cambodian Protected Areas. Eliminate all forms of hunting that are prohibited by law and stop illegal wildlife trade."
Mr. Seng Bunra, Senior Technical Director of Conservation International (CI), said: “The data show that the risk of using traps to catch wildlife in the forests of Cambodia today will turn into Forests where wildlife is no longer present. "The only way to save the wildlife is to stop the demand and trade in wildlife."
"Overall, promoting the participation of people, especially local communities, is fundamental to ending trapping and wildlife trade," said RICOF Representative in Cambodia. "This can be done by improving livelihoods, capacity building on legal rights and regulations related to natural resources, as well as raising awareness, as we have done today."
The Zero Trap campaign will run for six months in the provinces of Stung Treng, Preah Vihear, Kratie, Mondulkiri, Kampong Thom and Ratanakkiri. Launch Zero Trap Campaign in Kratie Province! 5 restaurant owners declare not to use wild meat for cooking and selling to customers
(Kratie): The Ministry of Environment, in collaboration with Kratie Provincial Administration and partner organizations, on June 18, 2022, continued to organize a zero trapping campaign in Kratie province, one of the six target provinces, to stop all kinds of traps in protected areas. Cambodia aims to conserve wildlife for future generations and to eliminate all kinds of traps in all protected areas throughout Cambodia.
This is the fourth time that the Zero Trap Campaign has been organized out of eight campaigns, the first of which was organized by the Ministry of Environment in Phnom Penh on March 3. The second campaign in Stung Treng was organized by WWF and the third campaign was recently completed by WCS in Preah Vihear.
The main goals of the "Zero Trap Campaign" are to serve four main purposes, including:
* First: Threats posed by trapping * Second: The need for effective law enforcement in protected areas * Third: The importance of reducing the demand for wildlife meat * Fourth: Participation Together with law enforcement authorities at the provincial level to combat trapping and illegal wildlife trafficking.
With the launch of the Trap Zero campaign in Kratie province, five restaurant owners have come out and vowed not to use wild meat to cook for their guests. In addition to the owners of the five restaurants, students at Hun Sen High School in Kratie City have pledged to bring their understanding of the benefits of natural resources and wildlife to their families, relatives and friends.
The WWF said the campaign was motivated by threats posed by the need for effective law enforcement in protected areas and the importance of reducing wildlife demand. The campaign aims to work with provincial law enforcement authorities to combat trapping and illegal wildlife trafficking.
"Based on the initial results, I would like to dedicate the spirit and values of the campaign to be remembered and raised by the Cambodian people and all stakeholders to encourage further joint action," he said. "Ensure no traps in the Cambodian Protected Areas, eliminate all forms of hunting that are prohibited by law and stop illegal wildlife trade."
The senior official encouraged all citizens to participate in the protection of Cambodia's wildlife and natural resources to support participation in building a better local economy, especially through the development of ecotourism and for the benefit of the system. Ecology in Cambodia and globally.
Please be informed that the six-month zero trapping campaign in 6 provinces east of the Mekong River, including: Stung Treng, Kratie, Mondulkiri, Ratanakkiri, Preah Vihear and Kampong Thom, was initiated and led by the Ministry of Environment and partner organizations working in conservation.
