Thai baht depreciates, boosts Thai economy by 0.4%
BANGKOK: The baht depreciation is expected to boost the Thai economy and boost GDP by 0.4%, but not all manufacturers will benefit, the Thai Ministry of Industry said. . According to the Bangkok Post.
Industries such as steel, pharmaceuticals and oil, which are heavily dependent on imports of raw materials and low exports, will face the burden of a weaker baht, the Bangkok Post reported.
The baht depreciated to 34.46 against the US dollar on June 7 from 33.38 baht on December 30 last year. The decline has made Thai exports cheaper, but imported raw materials have become more expensive.
Real GDP is a measure of economic activity after deducting inflation.
According to this calculation model, with this depreciation level, the export value in baht will increase by 2.14%, while the import price will increase by only 1.94%, consumption will increase by 0.21% and investment Will increase by 0.31% as manufacturers who benefit from additional exports will receive higher revenues.
But manufacturers with high raw material imports and low exports need to be cautious as the baht depreciates.
"They need to implement appropriate risk management to counter currency fluctuations by establishing foreign exchange contracts and foreign exchange rate offenses," said Lt. Gen. Rath Sreang. Defend security and order at the ASEAN Defense Ministers' Meeting (Video inside)
