Mr. Neth Pheaktra, Secretary of State of the Ministry of Environment, emphasized that this annual vulture census is to know the evolution of the number of vultures in Cambodia to facilitate management and put in place measures to protect rare species. This.
The Secretary of State said that the 2021 vulture census data shows that the three species of vultures in Cambodia, fire vultures, brown vultures and ash vultures, are present in a total of 121 in four vulture restaurants in protected areas. Cambodian nature, including 20 fire vultures, 66 gray vultures and 35 brown vultures.
In addition, a senior official of the Ministry of Environment pointed out that the results of the count of vultures by the vulture canteen in the rich wildlife sanctuary of the Mekong submerged forest in 2021 last year and showed that the number of vultures The total number of three species has doubled for the region, from 20 to 41 for the period from the end of June to the end of August 2021.
The Secretary of State for the Ministry of Environment said that Cambodia was the last hope for these vultures in Southeast Asia, but that number had halved in the past 10 years. In order to protect and conserve vultures, vulture research and conservation activities in Cambodia began in 2004, when the number of vultures recorded at the time of the census was 162, the first census data.
Eam Sam Onn, WWF Biodiversity Research and Monitoring Manager, said the annual vulture census was conducted five times in March, June, September and December. For June, two censuses are conducted to get accurate information because the vulture spawning and hatching seasons are between April and May.
Mr. Seng Teak, Director of WWF Cambodia, said that in recent years, the wildlife sanctuary area has been visited by vultures during the rainy season and concluded that the area, which is under conservation, protection and regular monitoring, is safe. As for the vultures, they came to live in this area. He said that through this supplementary feeding, the location at the sanctuary became more attractive to vultures.
In efforts to conserve vultures, the Secretary of State for the Ministry of Environment stated that the situation of Cambodian vultures is positive under the efforts of the Cambodian Vulture Conservation Working Group, but requires more protection. With the results of the last month, we see that the vulture canteen at Sambo Wildlife Sanctuary has seen a significant increase in the number of vultures.
Mr. Neth Pheaktra commended the research team of the Ministry of Environment and all partner organizations of the Cambodian Vulture Conservation Working Group, as well as all park officials who are performing their duties in the protected areas to protect and conserve natural habitats. Of wildlife sanctuaries where vultures are present.
