The presence of deer, a rare species in Cambodia, gives hope for conservation in Cambodia and the world
(Kratie): A 2022 research report on the status and distribution of deer in the Prek Prosop Wildlife Sanctuary of the Mekong Floodplain in Kratie province shows a total of 84 deer.
The results of this study also show the presence of adolescent deer and many cubs, which is a positive sign of the reproduction of this rare species in the wild.
Scientists have speculated that the deer became extinct in Cambodia until 2006, when it was rediscovered in Kratie province through automatic camera footage.
Eam Sam Onn, WWF Director of the Mekong Biodiversity Survey and Monitoring Unit, described: “It has been 16 years since the reintroduction of deer was compiled in Cambodia. "First for the conservation of this rare species in the world."
Deer are as difficult to spot and observe as other toenails. The study of the status and distribution of this species was carried out by deploying automatic cameras in the area where it lives. The research was co-organized by a team of wildlife experts from the Kratie Provincial Department of Environment, Ministry of Environment, WWF, BINCO, and involved community members and local authorities.
In addition to being present in Cambodia, the deer also live in India, Nepal, Bhutan, Bangladesh, Burma and Thailand, and scientists believe the deer may have become extinct in China, Laos and Vietnam.
Ms. Eng Sokhon, a member of the Volunteer Patrol Community in the Chroy Banteay Community of the Prek Prosop Wildlife Sanctuary, described: And release traps in the Prek Prasop Wildlife Sanctuary.
She said: "I, like other members of the patrol team, have the courage to participate in protecting wildlife, patrolling, removing traps from the forest, forest protection for wildlife, how to take care of wildlife and natural resources for future generations. Going forward.
Deer are listed on the IUCN Red List as globally endangered. Deer survival is threatened by habitat loss, poaching and the trap crisis caused by the illegal wildlife trade.
Mr. Seng Teak, Director of WWF, said: “The results of this research have not only highlighted Cambodia's reputation on the international stage, but also highlighted the global importance of the Mekong landscape as an important sanctuary. "For the survival of wildlife, both on land and in water of all kinds."
Additional urgent joint conservation measures are needed to ensure that the endangered deer sanctuary is not threatened by illegal activities, such as no trapping, no hunting and no wildlife trade in the area. Restoring deer numbers and habitats, as well as continuing to strengthen the livelihoods of local communities, is also a necessary rule that all stakeholders must be involved in preparing and implementing.
Researchers will continue to strengthen the scientific data on deer breeding by using advanced drone technology with thermal cameras throughout the sanctuary.
The Prek Prosop Wildlife Sanctuary, a deer sanctuary, is strictly protected by WWF-backed rangers, supported by WWF and community patrols.
(Phnom Penh): The Air Force has launched a training course for second-generation officers at the grassroots level.
The opening ceremony took place on June 16, 2022 at Pochentong Air Force Base, presided over by General Soeng Samnang, Deputy Commander of the Royal Cambodian Armed Forces and Commander of the Air Force. The ceremony was also attended by the Deputy Commander of the Air Force, representatives of the Training Command, the Commander-in-Chief, representatives of the Air Defense Command, the Commander-in-Chief of the Armed Forces and the second generation of trainees.
According to the report of the Director of the Air Force Training School, Brigadier General Yem Bun Reth, the second generation of trainees consisted of 30 officers from all units under the Air Force Command and 14 officers who have experience in the Air Force. Over the years, he has presented a wide range of documents, including management strategies, history, air force, education, psychology, philosophy, defense books, joint military knowledge, economics, politics, etc.
In this training, participants will be trained in theory and exercises for three months from June to September. General Soeng Samnang instructed the trainees to work hard to absorb the content of the lessons presented by the military officers and to have a strong and strong internal unity, to respect the rules and regulations in order to train to become the next unit leader.
The General also clarified and clarified to the trainees to know what is the Air Force? What are the missions of the Air Force in the air defense process? What are the factors of the Air Force mission to ensure the protection of the airspace from enemy encroachment?
Based on the experienced interpretation, the participants were deeply interested in the valuable presentation of General Soeng Samnang for training the new generation of human resources, especially the officers in turn.
After that, the officers representing the second generation of trainees became interested and committed to study hard and learn from the good experiences of military officers, discipline, solidarity and good relations with their peers. They expressed their deep gratitude to the Commander-in-Chief, the Deputy Commander-in-Chief of the Air Force for authorizing and supporting the training program for officers, bases, and commanders, who are important human resources in the field of defense.
In that program, General Soeng Samnang donated money to the second generation of trainees to meet the needs of the training in a happy atmosphere.
