Ministry of Environment Disseminates Research Report 2022 on Status and Deer Deer in Prek Prosop Wildlife Sanctuary in Kratie Province
(Kratie): The Ministry of Environment on June 16, 2022 announced the release of the 2022 research report on the status and distribution of deer in the Prek Prosop Wildlife Sanctuary of the Mekong Flood Forest in Kratie Province. A total of 84 deer.
According to the report, the results of this study also show the presence of many adolescent deer and cubs, which is a positive sign of the reproduction of this rare species in the natural habitat.
According to Mr. Neth Pheaktra, Secretary of State and Spokesperson of the Ministry of Environment, "The Ministry of Environment is very happy and proud that the nature of Cambodia provides a safe haven for the survival of globally important species such as deer. Scientists have confirmed its presence and reproduction in the Prek Prosop Wildlife Sanctuary.
"The Ministry of Environment, in conjunction with WWF, is continuing its efforts to protect and conserve this rare deer by improving local livelihoods, educating, disseminating and enforcing the law to prevent it," he added. "Deforestation, all kinds of hunting and trapping of all kinds in protected areas ensure the survival of deer and other universally valuable wildlife in their natural habitat."
Mr. Chhit Kim On, Head of Deer Conservation Patrol, expressed his commitment to protect and protect the deer present in the Prek Prasop Wildlife Sanctuary for the benefit of the local people through attraction. Tourists visit deer in the area.
"She is proud that rare deer-like animals still exist in the natural forest of Kratie province, and she has been involved in forest patrols and trapping at the Prek Prosop Wildlife Sanctuary," she said.
He said: "I, like other members of the patrol team, have the courage to participate in protecting wildlife, patrolling, removing traps from the forest, forest protection for wildlife, how to take care of wildlife and natural resources for future generations. Going forward.
Mr. Seng Teak, Director of WWF, emphasized, "The results of this research have not only highlighted Cambodia's reputation on the international stage, but also highlighted the global importance of the Mekong landscape as an important sanctuary for conservation." "The survival of wildlife, both on land and in water of all kinds."
"WWF appreciates and thanks the efforts of the park rangers and law enforcement officials, with the active participation of local authorities and communities, to protect these wildlife areas for the benefit of the community," he added. Of nature and all generations.
