Stung Treng Provincial Administration Holds Annual Provincial Meeting on Education, Youth and Sports to Present Education Results for the 2020-2021 School Year
(Stung Treng): In the morning of June 16, 2022, the Department of Education, Youth and Sports of Stung Treng Province organized the opening of the annual Provincial Youth and Sports Education Conference for the academic year 2020-2021 to review and present the results of educational work. 1 year ago and set the direction of education, youth and sports in the province 2021-2022.
The conference was chaired by Mr. Khan Van, Deputy Director of the Ministry of Education, Youth and Sports, and was attended by the Deputy Director of the Provincial Department of Education, Youth and Sports, the Director of the District Education Office, the Director of Public and Private Educational Institutions. Provinces and representatives of some development partners.
Mr. Pok Saroeun, Deputy Director of the Provincial Department of Education, Youth and Sports, said that in the academic year 2020-2021, this is the year the world has met with the Kovid-19 crisis, which has spread globally. The disease has hampered learning in 245 schools across Stung Treng province and affected 3,518 students, 17611 of whom are female.
In response to this impact, the Department of Education, Youth and Sports, in collaboration with the Office of Education, Youth and Sports of the District Administration, principals, teachers and relevant authorities, development partners, has worked hard to develop mechanisms for teaching in various forms, such as distance learning, teaching. Online, batch learning, assignment of assignments to students, and face-to-face meetings of teachers throughout the province.
Mr. Khan Van expressed his appreciation and appreciation for the educational work of Stung Treng Provincial Department of Education, Youth and Sports.
He said that according to the report of the Department of Education, the education sector has undergone significant development in terms of infrastructure, materials, as well as the quality of education through the implementation of reform strategies in the field of education, youth and sports, focusing on the five pillars.
Mr. Khan Van highlighted that the five pillars of the education sector in Cambodia include: First: the development of action plans, teachers' policies, second: the review of the curriculum, textbooks and school environment improvement, and third: the implementation. Inspection 4: Strengthening the tuition fees, such as national, regional and international assessments, and 5th: Higher education reform.
In order to achieve the reform of the education system, Mr. Khan Van advised all educators, especially teachers and principals to strengthen the management of schools to have good discipline, especially teachers.
