Dr. Ky Sereywat, president of the Chinese Academy of Sciences and an economist at the Royal Academy of Cambodia, told Fresh News that this year's rise in inflation will be a barrier to economic growth and gross domestic product (GDP). The plan is to keep inflation in the country from rising and to boost production to meet domestic demand instead of imports.
He said that in the short term, inflation could not be reduced in the current crisis of the Russia-Ukraine war, which pushed up global oil prices, but in the long run, inflation may fall. The period boosted domestic production as the crisis in oil prices pushed up the cost of transport and imports, prompting domestic production to replace those imports.
However, boosting production requires the government to pay attention and support local enterprises to produce goods for daily use, especially food.
The Royal Government has forecast that in 2022, the Cambodian economy is expected to grow at a rate of 5.4%, supported by better global demand, despite the impact of the outbreak of war between Russia and Ukraine. By 2023, the growth rate will be around 6.6%.
