Ministry of Environment and WWF hold two-day press visit to Mekong floodplain
(Kratie): A total of 30 journalists from 17 media outlets are taking a two-day press visit to the Mekong River flooded forest area in Kratie province from June 16-17, 2022. Co-organized by the Ministry of Environment and WWF.
According to Mr. Neth Pheaktra, Secretary of State and Spokesperson of the Ministry of Environment, who led the visit, the purpose of the visit was to provide journalists with the opportunity to experience first-hand the wildlife situation in the area and explore the potential of the forest area. Mekong flooding for the conservation of biodiversity and the restoration of important wildlife species of global value to contribute to the economic development and livelihoods of local communities.
Mr. Neth Pheaktra said, "The purpose of this press visit is in accordance with the policy of the Ministry of Environment, which is to promote and increase professional reporting on the environment, natural resources and biodiversity conservation in Cambodia."
"The information that people receive will help increase their environmental awareness and contribute to cultivating a love and care for the environment, natural resources and biodiversity, not only can encourage them to take positive action to participate," he said. Protect these resources for future generations as well. ”
Mr. Seng Teak, Director of WWF, emphasized the importance of the press visit as bringing closer links between journalism and science to increase the efficiency and quality of professional dissemination of conservation information to the public and stakeholders. This sector.
Prek Prosop Wildlife Sanctuary covers a total area of 12,770 hectares in Prek Prosop District. Sambo Wildlife Sanctuary covers a total area of 50,093 hectares in Sambo District, Kratie Province.
These two wildlife sanctuaries are important natural habitats for a total of 11 species of mammals, 56 species of reptiles and reptiles, 683 species of plants, more than 200 species of birds and 223 species of fish.
In addition, non-timber forest and river resources are important sources of livelihoods and livelihoods for local communities, as well as providing support systems for resilience to climate change, floods and droughts. We are grateful for the funding and support of WWF Belgium, Government of Belgium (DGD), Major Ecosystem Partnership Fund (CEPF), Swiss Agency for Development and Cooperation (SDC) and Binco.
