Commentary: Cambodian Agriculture Officials and Longan Farmers Expect Highly in Chinese Market
(Phnom Penh): Cambodia's Pailin or Tangen fruit will start to be imported directly to China in the coming harvest season, between September or October 2022, according to officials from the Ministry of Agriculture.
Ke Monivuth, director of the Department of Crop Protection and Hygiene of the General Department of Agriculture of the Ministry of Agriculture, Forestry and Fisheries, recently told Cambodia-China Friendship Radio by telephone that more than 60 gourd farms have been planted. Registered with the Ministry of Agriculture for export to China in accordance with the technical standards agreed with the General Administration of Customs recently.
Mr. Ke Monivuth added that it does not only refer to Pailin lentils, but also lentils in other provinces such as Battambang, Pursat and Banteay Meanchey. At the same time, Mr. Tam Sophoan, Member of the Board of Directors of Pailin Longan Agricultural Community in charge of Administration, told Cambodia-China Friendship Radio that the farmers in the community were very happy to hear about the news of longan export. Go to China directly.
As a farmer and on behalf of the Pailin Longan Agricultural Community, Mr. Tam Sophoan would like to thank the Chinese side for helping to buy longan fruit from Cambodia, which has given the market greater confidence. Helping to buy longan fruit from the Chinese side really contributes to improving the livelihood of Cambodian farmers is a great value.
For Cambodia today, the provinces with the largest number of farmers growing longan are Pailin, Battambang, Ratanakkiri and Banteay Meanchey. Among these cultivated areas of Pailin, there are nearly 7,000 hectares, which farmers will produce 110,000 tons in 2021, according to the report of the Ministry of Agriculture.
The nationwide oil shortage in Southeast Asia, which has a population of 7.5 million, is the latest bad sign due to rising oil prices and a weakening currency. The most worrying thing is the debt burden, which reduces reserves, a challenge for this communist country.
According to the World Bank, as of December last year, Laos had $ 1.3 billion in foreign exchange reserves, while total foreign debt repayments were the same amount every year until 2025, which is about half of the total. Gross domestic income.
US interest rate hikes have devalued local currencies against the dollar and pushed up the price of imported goods, including oil, largely due to Russia's invasion of Ukraine. This has led to high debt burden in developing countries and declining revenues and insufficient cash reserves.
All of these factors pushed Sri Lanka into an economic and political crisis earlier this year, making it unable to import fuel, food and medicine, failing to pay its debts and seeking foreign funding.
The increase in public debt in Laos last year reached $ 14.5 billion, about half of China's debt, according to the World Bank. Part of the country's debt obligations are loans to fund 30 percent of its $ 5.9 billion China-Laos railway project, which launched in December last year.
Depreciation has hampered importers seeking to buy enough fuel for the domestic market, causing huge supply gaps and long queues at gas stations. Local media reported in May that Laos received less than half of the 120 million liters of fuel needed per month to meet public demand.
Although the Lao economy is projected to grow 3.8% this year, it depends on a successful debt renegotiation, according to a recent World Bank report. . Moody's downgrades Laos' credit rating to reflect severe government liquidity tensions and low foreign exchange reserves in 2020
