KOICA Provides $ 10 Million to Implement Peace Building Project through Diversified Rural Community Development
(Phnom Penh): The Korea International Cooperation Agency (KICA) provides $ 10 million to develop communities in northwestern Cambodia to become a peaceful and prosperous community. This confirmation was made during the signing ceremony of the minutes of discussions with the Ministry of Rural Development on the project "Building a Peace Village through the development of diverse rural communities".
The minutes of the discussion were signed by Mr. Rho Hyunjun, Chief Representative of KOICA Cambodia and Dr. Ouk Rabun, Minister of Rural Development, on 9 June 2022 at the Ministry Headquarters.
The project will be implemented in three provinces: Battambang, Pailin and Banteay Meanchey, which have been hit by landmines and unexploded ordnance left over from the war. This project aims to develop the Northwest region sustainably and peacefully through an environmentally friendly rural community development program.
Dr. Ouk Rabun said the project was a major support as the areas were heavily war-torn and contained landmines and unexploded ordnance. After the end of the war, the government did its best to address those issues in cooperation with development partners and civil society organizations. As a result, most landmines and unexploded ordnance in the area have been cleared and destroyed.
However, these areas still need assistance as they lack access to clean water, social and economic infrastructure, especially support for agricultural activities to use land effectively.
"Rural development is a pillar of the agency's national partnership strategy," said Rushan Hean Chun, chief representative of the Cambodian Customs Agency. KOICA is committed to supporting this sector. KOICA is pleased to implement this significant project to contribute to the socio-economic development of northwestern Cambodia. ”
