Hand, foot and mouth disease is a viral infection that affects many babies and children, especially children under the age of 10, especially children under five. This disease has been present in our country since 2012 and is also a disease that occurs in many countries around the world.
In a small number of children, blisters on the hands, feet, and mouth can cause serious illness. Symptoms of a serious illness include neurological symptoms (drowsiness, weakness of the limbs, and seizures or cramps) and shortness of breath (fatigue). If you see any of these symptoms on your child, parents or guardians should seek immediate medical attention at the nearest hospital.
Yes! Because blisters on the hands, feet and mouth are caused by different viruses. Outbreaks appear to be exacerbated during pregnancy and in children with defective immune systems.
In addition, the Ministry of Health would like to remind and call on all citizens to continue to increase vigilance by consistently implementing the following measures:
Wash your hands thoroughly before preparing food, before eating, before feeding the baby or child, after going to the toilet and after changing the baby's diaper or wiping the child's body. Clean surfaces and objects contaminated with water and soap. * Avoid close contact (kissing, hugging, sharing utensils, etc.) Continue to provide nutritious food and clean water to children. Use separate spoons and plates. Separately for each child.
Babysitters need to have good personal hygiene. If you have a health problem or illness or suspect an infection, please hurry to seek medical advice at a public hospital or contact the number provided below.
