Commentary: Tackling Global Food Security Issues China Efforts on Its Own
BEIJING (Reuters) - The World Food Program (WFP) recently issued a warning that the world could face "the worst food crisis after World War II" with a population of 1.7 billion. It has been affected by the destruction of the food, energy and financial systems and is exacerbating the problem of poverty and hunger.
The issue of global food security is gaining more and more attention from the people in the age of globalization, the food crisis has no limits, so no country can escape alone. As a country of more than 1.4 billion people, China attaches great importance to agricultural production and food security. China is now the world's largest food producer and third largest food exporter.
Recently, a small number of Western countries have criticized China for stockpiling food in international markets. Foreign Ministry spokesman Wang Wenbin responded that China has the capacity And confident enough to hold a plate of Chinese people firmly in their hands, no need to stockpile food in the international market.
Since the outbreak of Kovid 19, China has been active in responding to the initiatives of international organizations such as the United Nations, which have provided emergency food aid to Afghanistan, Sri Lanka, Kenya, Uganda, Liberia and others. In the field of global food governance, China has always contributed to China's wisdom, Chinese projects and Chinese forces in resolving global food security issues.
