Speaking to Fresh News on June 6, Mr. Vy Veasna, Assistant of the Kampong Speu Palm Sugar Association, said that the export process of Kampong Speu palm sugar this year is still good, while only the Russian and Indian markets can not. Exported due to the war between Russia and Ukraine.
He said that so far, some markets need about 150 tons of powdered palm sugar. However, he worries that production to supply these markets may not meet market demand due to weather conditions, which so far has produced only 57 tonnes of powdered palm sugar for export. However, he expects production to reach 100 tonnes in the near future.
Responding to the growing demand, he said the association would expand production, especially encouraging them to produce more sugar for export than meat sugar by increasing the number of producers (palm growers). Last year, the number of producers was 150, while this year the number of members of Kampong Speu palm sugar producers has increased to 155. He said that the production of all types of palm sugar this year is around 150 to 170 tons.
Along with the increasing demand, the price of Kampong Speu palm sugar this year has also increased more than last year. This year, palm sugar can be sold from 7,000 riel to 7,100 riel per kilogram, a slight increase from last year, at 6,800 riel per kilogram, while meat sugar was priced at 6,500 riel, while in previous years it was sold at a price between From 5,000 to 5,500 riel per kilogram.
At the same time, he expects that the export of Kampong Speu palm sugar, a GI brand to Russia, will be possible next year, while this year Kampong Speu palm sugar exports to Russia and India will be blocked. Stagnant due to the war between Russia and Ukraine.
He said that at the end of 2021, the Indian side negotiated to buy about 100 tons of palm sugar Kampong Speu and early 2022 Russia negotiated to buy about 50 tons of palm sugar from Cambodia. However, due to the war between Russia and Ukraine, the export process to the two countries was suspended.
Kampong Speu Palm Sugar was recognized as a Geographical Indication (GI) product in 2010 and registered as a Protected Geographical Indication (PGI) by the European Union on April 3, 2019, which is recognized as a symbol of Cambodia. Kampong Speu palm sugar production takes place in three areas: Samrong Tong and Oudong districts, Kampong Speu province, and another area in Ang Snoul district, Kandal province.
In 2021, the export of Kampong Speu GI palm sugar was 157 tons. Kampong Speu palm sugar is exported through companies, mostly to the European Union, Canada, Germany, France, China, Korea. The local supply is meat sugar.
