Outbreaks of chickenpox have not yet affected tourists' travel to Cambodia
(Phnom Penh): Tourism experts have found that outbreaks of chickenpox in some countries in the world have not affected the travel of national and international tourists because the disease is not contagious. The airway.
Ho Vandy, an adviser to the Cambodian Tourism Association, said that the outbreak of foot-and-mouth disease was not a problem for the development of tourists and other activities, as there were currently 20 countries infected with foot-and-mouth disease. With only about 500 people infected, chickenpox can be considered a minor disease and not as contagious as Kovid 19.
"Chickenpox is not a disease that poses a risk to national and international travel," he said. In the case of transmission or transmission into Cambodia to domestic tourists does not cause any side effects, because chickenpox is not transmitted through the respiratory tract, face-to-face contact like the flu or Kovid 19. "This can not stop the movement as well as domestic tourism."
Mr. Ho Vandy stated that currently, Cambodian tourism is slowly recovering through the wide opening of the border between Cambodia, Thailand and Cambodia-Vietnam, which benefits the three countries, including Cambodia-Thailand and Vietnam, with Cambodian tourism. To Vietnam, Vietnamese-Thai tourists came to Cambodia through the reopening of the gates. This is a win-win strategy and a culture of revenue sharing and economic continuity.
In particular, Thuon Sinan, President of the Asia-Pacific Tourism Association, said, "Currently, we can not see the impact of chickenpox on tourism, but if the disease continues to develop, it may affect tourism again. "Because tourism is like rotten wood, not very strong."
