Readers' opinion: It is not right to say that Khmer is poor!
Certainly not. If so, what arguments or indicators do we have to serve as a basis for measuring poverty and to justify the above question?
However, given the recent changes in the socio-economic situation, Cambodia has redefined the poverty line using the latest Cambodian Socio-Economic Survey data for 2019/2020, and the current national poverty line in Cambodia is equal. 10,951 riels or US $ 2.7 per person per day. With this new poverty line, about 18% of the Cambodian population is defined as poor.
Sometimes when we talk about numbers and statistics, we can find it difficult to understand, and sometimes the economic situation changes, we sometimes find it difficult to understand when we only look at what lies ahead. The rise of the economy and the living standards of the people have to be compared backwards to know how far we have come and how much we have progressed.
It is common in any country, rich or poor. And in a free market economy and capitalism, it is justified that economic opportunities are unequal, and that is why governments of all countries are constantly levying taxes and attracting investment from factories or development projects to rural areas to share the benefits. Occurs from economic growth.
If we want absolute equality, as long as we use the dictatorial communist plan economy, then we are all equal, no rich, no poor. Simply put, all are equally poor. Or, if you want equality, perfect justice, until you act like Pol Pot, take all the rich people to farm or kill them at once.
Through the above discussion, I just want to provide some capital of discussion, and I hope that readers can consider for themselves the claim that Khmer is getting poorer and poorer the gap is getting bigger and bigger. Is that the right thing to say? Please consider whether such a statement is fair for the history of Cambodia's economic and social progress.
